Now I have the full context. I've already read the test file, the project file, and the .NET extension. Let me produce the formal audit using the testsmells.org 19-smell catalog, calibrated for integration tests.

---

## Test Smell Audit — `UserRepositoryIntegrationTests.cs`

**Framework:** MSTest v4 · **.NET 10** · **Integration test** (class name + `[TestCategory("Integration")]`)

### Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| Critical | 1 | 1 |
| High | 2 | 2 |
| Medium | 1 | 1 |
| Low | 0 | 0 |
| **Total** | **4** | **4** |

**3 of 7 tests are clean** — `InsertUser_PersistsToDatabase`, `UpdateUser_ModifiesExistingRecord`, and `DeleteUser_RemovesFromDatabase` are well-structured with clear AAA separation, specific assertions, and descriptive names. `ListUsers_ReturnsAllInserted` and `InsertUser_ConcurrentInserts_MaintainsIntegrity` are also solid (counts are self-documenting from the arrange phase).

---

### Critical Findings

#### 1. Assertion Roulette / Unknown Test — `BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors` (line 113)

**Formal smell:** *Unknown Test* (testsmells.org §4)

The test executes 100 inserts but contains **zero assertions**. It passes as long as no exception is thrown — a silently corrupted database would go undetected.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — exercises code, verifies nothing
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_RunsWithoutErrors()
{
    for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));
}
```
```csharp
// ✅ Fix — assert the observable outcome
[TestMethod]
public void BulkInsert_PersistsAllRecords()
{
    const int count = 100;
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++)
        _repository.Insert(new User($"bulk{i}@example.com", $"Bulk User {i}"));

    var users = _repository.ListAll();
    Assert.AreEqual(count, users.Count);
}
```

---

### High Findings

#### 2. Sleepy Test — `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 82)

**Formal smell:** *Sleepy Test* (testsmells.org §18)

`Thread.Sleep(3000)` is a hard-coded wall-clock wait. Even in an integration test, a fixed sleep is unreliable — too short and it flakes, too long and it slows the suite. Additionally, the method is `async Task` but uses a synchronous blocking call.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — fixed 3-second sleep, blocks the thread
Thread.Sleep(3000);
Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id));
```
```csharp
// ✅ Fix — poll with a timeout using async
[TestMethod]
public async Task NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay()
{
    var user = new User("dave@example.com", "Dave");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var timeout = TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
    var interval = TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(200);
    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();
    while (sw.Elapsed < timeout)
    {
        if (_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id))
            break;
        await Task.Delay(interval);
    }

    Assert.IsTrue(_repository.WasNotificationSent(user.Id),
        "Notification was not sent within the timeout.");
}
```

#### 3. Conditional Test Logic — `GetUser_ReturnsCorrectType` (line 94)

**Formal smell:** *Conditional Test Logic* (testsmells.org §1)

The `if/else` branch means **only one path is verified per run**, and a silent type change would shift the test to the other branch without any failure.

```csharp
// ❌ Current — branching hides which path actually ran
if (loaded is PremiumUser premium)
    Assert.IsTrue(premium.DiscountRate > 0);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded!.Name);
```
```csharp
// ✅ Fix — split into two deterministic tests
[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_StandardUser_ReturnsCorrectName()
{
    var user = new User("eve@example.com", "Eve");
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("eve@example.com");

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.AreEqual("Eve", loaded.Name);
}

[TestMethod]
public void GetUser_PremiumUser_HasPositiveDiscount()
{
    var user = new PremiumUser("vip@example.com", "VIP", discountRate: 0.1m);
    _repository.Insert(user);

    var loaded = _repository.GetByEmail("vip@example.com") as PremiumUser;

    Assert.IsNotNull(loaded);
    Assert.IsTrue(loaded.DiscountRate > 0);
}
```

---

### Medium Findings

#### 4. Magic Number Test — `NotifyOnInsert_SendsEventAfterDelay` (line 87)

**Formal smell:** *Magic Number Test* (testsmells.org §12)

The literal `3000` in `Thread.Sleep(3000)` has no explanation. Why 3 seconds? Is the notification service SLA 2s? 5s? A named constant communicates intent (addressed by the polling fix above via an explicit `timeout` variable).

> **Integration-test calibration:** The count literals `3` (line 76) and `10` (line 132) are **not** flagged — they directly match the number of items inserted in the same test and are self-documenting.

---

### Smells Scanned — No Issues Found

| # | testsmells.org Smell | Result |
|---|---------------------|--------|
| 5 | Assertion Roulette | ✅ Clean — assertion counts per test are reasonable (2–4) and diagnostically clear |
| 6 | Duplicate Assert | ✅ Clean — no redundant repeated assertions |
| 7 | Eager Test | ✅ Downgraded — multi-method calls are appropriate for integration scope |
| 8 | Sensitive Equality | ✅ Clean — no `ToString()` comparisons |
| 9 | Exception Handling | ✅ Clean — no `try`/`catch` blocks in tests |
| 10 | General Fixture | ✅ Clean — `_connection` and `_repository` are used by every test |
| 11 | Mystery Guest | ✅ Downgraded — `SqliteConnection(":memory:")` is an in-memory fake, explicitly allowed |
| 12 | Ignored Test | ✅ Clean — no `[Ignore]` annotations |
| 13 | Resource Optimism | ✅ Clean — in-memory DB, no external resource assumptions |
| 14 | Redundant Assertion | ✅ Clean — no `Assert.IsTrue(true)` or tautologies |
| 15 | Default Test | ✅ Clean — no auto-generated stubs |
| 16 | Constructor Initialization | ✅ Clean — uses `[TestInitialize]`, not constructor |
| 17 | Lazy Test | ✅ Clean — each test verifies a distinct operation |
| 18 | Verbose Test (Print Statement) | ✅ Clean — no `Console.WriteLine` / `Debug.WriteLine` |
| 19 | Duplicate Test | ✅ Clean — no near-identical test bodies |

---

### Positive Observations

- **`sealed class`** — follows MSTest v4 best practice
- **`[TestCategory("Integration")]`** — properly tagged for selective filtering
- **`[TestCleanup]` disposes the connection** — no resource leaks
- **Clear AAA structure** in the 3 clean tests with whitespace separating phases
- **Descriptive names** follow `Method_Scenario` convention consistently
- **In-memory SQLite** — avoids Mystery Guest by keeping the database hermetic

---

### Prioritized Remediation

| Priority | Smell | Fix | Effort |
|----------|-------|-----|--------|
| 🔴 1 | Unknown Test (`BulkInsert`) | Add count assertion | ~2 min |
| 🟠 2 | Conditional Test Logic (`GetUser`) | Split into 2 tests | ~5 min |
| 🟠 3 | Sleepy Test (`NotifyOnInsert`) | Replace sleep with polling loop | ~10 min |

**Bottom line:** 4 of 7 tests are well-designed. The 3 problematic tests each have a single clear fix. After remediation this will be a solid integration test suite.
